10
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:21,373
CARLA:

11
00:00:21,373 --> 00:00:21,774
OK Rob.

12
00:00:21,774 --> 00:00:27,286
I'm glad we chose quite a specific topic - cities built next to the sea.

13
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:31,000
It made it much easier to find relevant information.

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:31,357
ROB:

15
00:00:31,357 --> 00:00:31,714
Yeah.

16
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:36,354
And cities are growing so quickly - I mean, we know that more than

17
00:00:36,354 --> 00:00:39,210
half the world's population lives in cities now.

18
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:39,582
CARLA:

19
00:00:39,582 --> 00:00:43,675
Yeah, though that's all cities, not just ones on the coast.

20
00:00:43,675 --> 00:00:48,140
But most of the biggest cities are actually built by the sea.

21
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,000
I'd not realised that before.

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:50,321
ROB:

23
00:00:50,321 --> 00:00:50,964
Nor me.

24
00:00:50,964 --> 00:00:55,464
And what's more, a lot of them are built at places where rivers come

25
00:00:55,464 --> 00:00:56,750
out into the sea.

26
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:59,000
But apparently this can be a problem.

27
00:00:59,000 --> 00:00:59,695
CARLA:

28
00:00:59,695 --> 00:01:00,390
Why?

29
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:00,810
ROB:

30
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:06,684
Well, as the city expands, agriculture and industry tend to spread further inland along

31
00:01:06,684 --> 00:01:07,524
the rivers,

32
00:01:07,524 --> 00:01:11,720
and so agriculture moves even further inland up the river.

33
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:17,394
That's not necessarily a problem, except it means more and more pollutants are discharged

34
00:01:17,394 --> 00:01:18,610
into the rivers.

35
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:19,084
CARLA:

36
00:01:19,084 --> 00:01:22,880
So these are brought downstream to the cities?

37
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:23,246
ROB:

38
00:01:23,246 --> 00:01:23,613
Right.

39
00:01:23,613 --> 00:01:23,979
Hmm.

40
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:29,110
Did you read that article about Miami, on the east coast of the USA?

41
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:29,840
CARLA:

42
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:30,570
No.

43
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:30,936
ROB:

44
00:01:30,936 --> 00:01:36,065
Well, apparently back in the 1950s they built channels to drain away the water

45
00:01:36,065 --> 00:01:37,530
in case of flooding.

46
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:38,207
CARLA:

47
00:01:38,207 --> 00:01:39,560
Sounds sensible.

48
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:39,912
ROB:

49
00:01:39,912 --> 00:01:43,431
Yeah, they spent quite a lot of money on them.

50
00:01:43,431 --> 00:01:46,950
But what they didn't take into account was global warming.

51
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:50,750
So they built the drainage channels too close to sea level,

52
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:54,550
and now sea levels are rising, they're more or less useless.

53
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:59,539
If there's a lot of rain, the water can't run away, there's nowhere for

54
00:01:59,539 --> 00:02:00,608
it to go.

55
00:02:00,608 --> 00:02:02,390
The whole design was faulty.

56
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:02,863
CARLA:

57
00:02:02,863 --> 00:02:07,120
So what are the authorities doing about it now?

58
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:07,461
ROB:

59
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:08,483
I don't know.

60
00:02:08,483 --> 00:02:13,255
I did read that they're aiming to stop disposing of waste water into the

61
00:02:13,255 --> 00:02:15,300
ocean over the next ten years.

62
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:15,742
CARLA:

63
00:02:15,742 --> 00:02:20,160
But that won't help with flood prevention now, will it?

64
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:20,523
ROB:

65
00:02:20,523 --> 00:02:20,886
No.

66
00:02:20,886 --> 00:02:25,969
Really they just need to find the money for something to replace the drainage

67
00:02:25,969 --> 00:02:28,873
channels in order to protect against flooding now.

68
00:02:28,873 --> 00:02:33,230
But in the long term they need to consider the whole ecosystem.

69
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:33,652
CARLA:

70
00:02:33,652 --> 00:02:34,074
Right.

71
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:39,982
Really, though, coastal cities can't deal with their problems on their own, can they?

72
00:02:39,982 --> 00:02:45,890
I mean, they've got to start acting together at an international level instead of

73
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:48,000
just doing their own thing.

74
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:48,372
ROB:

75
00:02:48,372 --> 00:02:48,744
Absolutely.

76
00:02:48,744 --> 00:02:53,950
The thing is, everyone knows what the problems are and environmentalists have a pretty

77
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:57,669
good idea of what we should be doing about them,

78
00:02:57,669 --> 00:03:01,760
so they should be able to work together to some extent.

79
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:06,533
But it's going to be a long time before countries come to a decision

80
00:03:06,533 --> 00:03:09,260
on what principles they're prepared to abide by.

81
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:09,808
CARLA:

82
00:03:09,808 --> 00:03:12,550
Yes, if they ever do.

83
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:12,997
CARLA:

84
00:03:12,997 --> 00:03:17,463
So I think we've probably got enough for our presentation.

85
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:19,250
It's only fifteen minutes.

86
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:19,724
ROB:

87
00:03:19,724 --> 00:03:20,199
OK.

88
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:26,841
So I suppose we'll begin with some general historical background about why coastal cities

89
00:03:26,841 --> 00:03:27,790
